"Brings five linked strategic choices together to test whether a business has a coherent overall strategy.\n\nUse when: Review whether a proposed strategy states where and how to compete. Expose contradictions between positioning, entry methods, timing and economic logic.\n\nAvoid when: Only environmental scanning or individual project prioritization is required.\n\nVisual grammar: Five named regions represent arenas, vehicles, differentiators, staging and economic logic. Brief commitments describe choices within each region. A central purpose or visual arrangement emphasizes their joint coherence.\n\nBuild: State the strategic objective and scope of the business. Specify concrete choices in each of the five elements. Test whether the elements reinforce one another and identify unresolved tradeoffs.\n\nCheck: Arenas state meaningful market and activity boundaries. Vehicles describe how the business will reach those arenas. Economic logic explains how the chosen configuration can earn returns.\n\nPitfalls: Replacing choices with aspirations such as be the best. Writing five independent lists that conflict when implemented together.\n\nExample: A training company selects specialist employers, partner distribution, practical credentials, staged expansion and cohort economics as one coherent strategy.\n\nAlternatives: Business Model Canvas, Strategy map."
